            "costUsd": null,
            "finalAnswer": "When elements are deleted from the scene in Excalidraw (e.g., via the Delete/Backspace action), Excalidraw cleans up bindings that referenced those deleted elements by invoking the function `fixBindingsAfterDeletion`. This function is defined as follows:\n\n```typescript\nexport const fixBindingsAfterDeletion = (\n  sceneElements: readonly ExcalidrawElement[],\n  deletedElements: readonly ExcalidrawElement[],\n): void => {\n  const elements = arrayToMap(sceneElements);\n\n  for (const element of deletedElements) {\n    BoundElement.unbindAffected(elements, element, mutateElement);\n    BindableElement.unbindAffected(elements, element, mutateElement);\n  }\n};\n```\n\nHere's what happens in this cleanup process:\n\n1. It converts the current scene elements array into a map for quick lookup.\n2. For each deleted element, it calls `unbindAffected` on both `BoundElement` and `BindableElement`. These calls handle removing any bindings to the deleted element.\n3. The `unbindAffected` method presumably mutates (via `mutateElement`) the surviving elements that had bindings to the deleted elements, to remove or update those references so they don't dangle.\n\nIn summary, Excalidraw actively iterates over deleted elements and calls unbinding routines on reference holders to remove bindings to the deleted elements, ensuring no dangling references remain after deletion.",
